e images before SSD mini- ... bottom: image, rank transform, PyramLK OF, eFOLKI OF\nto pay is the large number of iterations needed by the\n\ndiffusion process for convergence. Finally, we have deci-\n\nded to retain the basic structure of FOLKI, which is very\n\nefﬁcient, and to add preprocessing and intermediate steps\n\nduring the iterations. This work is clearly in the line of [37]\n\non improvements of a HS (Horn–Schunck) OF algorithm.\n\nThe ﬁrst problem is that SSD is not a robust criterion,\n\nwhich implies that the motion estimation greatly depends\n\non the local image texture and fails in case of illumination\n\nchanges. Better results are obtained by using ZNCC, which\n\nis easy to implement with a slight approximation: we have\n\nused this version successfully for processing PIV images,\n\nsee Sect. 4.2 and Ref. [13]. However, for general videos,\n\nZNCC is not sufﬁciently robust. In this case, we apply a\n\nRankn transform [43] to the images before SSD mini-\n\nmization. Each pixel x is replaced by the number of\n\nneighboring pixels with an intensity lower than I ð x Þ.This\n\nFig. 3 Gain in robustness of eFOLKI over PyramLK. From top to\n\nbottom: image, rank transform, PyramLK OF, eFOLKI OF\n\nJ Real-Time Image Proc\n\n123\n\ntransform is fast and has only one parameter: the radius n\n\nof the neighborhood. Transformed images have a com-\n\npressed intensity range, which drastically increases the\n\nrobustness and homogeneity of the FOLKI result.\n\nThe second issue is related to convergence. In the case\n\nof large displacements, window-based methods such as\n\nFOLKI require a large radius to converge, but this leads to\n\na lower resolution of the estimated ﬂow. The solution\n\nproposed here is to vary the radius of the window during\n\nthe iterations. In practice only two or three values are\n\nsufﬁcient, starting from an initial large radius which pro-\n\nvides smooth and reliable estimation, and ending with a\n\nsmall value to get high-resolution details. This process\n\nincreases the number of iterations of the algorithm.\n\nThe resulting algorithm is called eFOLKI for ‘‘extended\n\nFOLKI’’. Figure 3 presents the eFOLKI OF estimate\n\n(bottom line): it appears much more precise and reliable\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDOCUMENT RESUME (https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED319774.pdf)\nciteturn9404search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 15.5 years ago; Games, P.A., & Lucas, P.A. ... The rank transform in the two-sample\nFeir-Walsh, B.J., & Toothaker, L.E.,1974). An empirical comparison of\nthe ANOVA F-test, normal scores test and Kruskal-Wallis test under\nviolation of assumptions.\nEducational and Psychological\nMeasurement, 34, 789-799.\nGames, P.A., & Lucas, P.A. (1966).\nThe analysis of variance of\nindependent groups on non-normal and normally transformed data.\nEducational and Psychological Measurement, 26, 311-327.\nKohr, R.L., & Games, P.A. (1974).\nRobustness of the analysis of\nvariance, the Welch procedure and a Box procedure to heterogeneous\nvariances.\nJournal of Experimental Education, 43, 61-69.\nLevine, D.W., & Dunlap, W.P. (1982).\nPower of the F test with skewed\ndata: should one transform or not?\nPsychological Bulletin, 92, 272-\n--.\n280.\nLin, L.I., & Sanford, R.L. (1983).\nThe robustness of the likelihood\nratio test, the nonparametric rank sum test, and F-ratio tests when\nthe populations are from the negative binom4 1 family.\nCommunication in Statistics: Simulation and Computation, 12,\n523-\n539.\nMcSweeney, M., & Penfield, D. (1969).\nThe normal scores test for the c-\nsample problem.\nBritish Journal of Mathematical and Statistical\nPsychology, 22, 177-192.\nNath, R., & Duran, B.S. (1981).\nThe rank transform in the two-sample\nlocation problem.\nCommunication in Statistics: Simulation and\nComputation, 10, 383-394.\n27\n30--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCombinatorics 79 Part 1 (Annals of Discrete Mathematics Vol 8) (https://ndl.ethernet.edu.et/bitstream/123456789/53847/1/M.%20Deza.pdf)\nciteturn9404search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 16.6 years ago; For ordered sets P and Q, Q is a retract of P if there are order-preserving maps f of Q to P and g of P to Q such that g∘f is the identity map of Q. ... (In Fig. 3 we illustrate a typical application of this concept: the retraction map of the ordered set onto the 6-crown [shaded elements] followed by the fixed point free automorphism of the 6-crown, yields an order-preserving map with no fixed points.)\nThe problem of fixed points in ordered sets 285\n\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\nFig. 2. Three ordered-set diagrams labeled:\n- A₂ — Antichain\n- C₂ₙ (n ≥ 2) — Crown\n- P∞ — Infinite Fence\n</visual_element>\n\nLet L be a complete lattice [that is, an ordered set in which every subset has supremum and infimum]. Let f be an order-preserving map of L to itself, set A = {x ∈ L | x ≤ f(x)}, and let a = sup A (cf. Fig. 1). Then x ≤ f(x) ≤ f(a) whenever x ∈ A and especially, a ≤ f(a). This follows that f(a) ≤ f(f(a)), whence f(a) ≤ a, that is, a is a fixed point of f.\n\n(b) Retractions. The fixed point property for ordered sets of length one [every chain has at most two elements] was first investigated by the author in [25] and later with R. Nowakowski in [22]: An ordered set P of length one has the fixed point property if and only if (i) P is connected, (ii) P contains no crowns, and (iii) P contains no infinite fence⁴ (see Fig. 2).\n\nWhile conceptually transparent this formulation conceals the central idea of its proof: retraction. For ordered sets P and Q, Q is a retract of P if there are order-preserving maps f of Q to P and g of P to Q such that g∘f is the identity map of Q. As Q is, in this case, isomorphic to a subset of P we may equivalently reformulate this concept as follows: a subset Q of P is a retract of P if there is an order-preserving map g of P to Q satisfying g|Q = idQ. We call g a retraction map. (In Fig. 3 we illustrate a typical application of this concept: the retraction map of the ordered set onto the 6-crown [shaded elements] followed by the fixed point free automorphism of the 6-crown, yields an order-preserving map with no fixed points.)\n\n<visual_element id=\"e2\">\nFig. 3. Three diagrams connected by “+” and “=”:\n- Left: “Retraction”\n- Middle: “Fixed Point Free Automorphism”\n- Right: “Fixed Point Free Map”\n</visual_element>\n\n⁴ The theme of “completeness” is here replaced by “crowns”. In fact, this result tends to confirm the impression that crowns should play a central role in the description of fixed point free ordered sets. While this role has not yet been precisely delineated a rather more general concept emerges as unmistakably essential: “retraction”.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBACHELOR THESIS (https://essay.utwente.nl/fileshare/file/78220/vanRaalte_BA_BMS.pdf)\nciteturn9404search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.2 years ago; Human Communication Research, ... L., Lucas, J. ... A power study of a rank transform for the two-way classification model when\nDouglas, W. (1990). Uncertainty, information‐seeking, and liking during initial interaction. Western \nJournal of Speech Communication, 54(1), 66-81. doi:10.1080/10570319009374325 \nDragojevic, M., & Giles, H. (2016). I Don't Like You Because You're Hard to Understand: The Role of \nProcessing Fluency in the Language Attitudes Process. Human Communication Research, \n42(3), 396-420. doi:10.1111/hcre.12079 \nGabel, L. L., Lucas, J. B., & Westbury, R. C. (1993). Why do patients continue to see the same \nphysician? The Family practice research journal, 13(2), 133-147.  \nHeller Baird, C., & Parasnis, G. (2011). From social media to social customer relationship \nmanagement. Strategy & Leadership, 39(5), 30–37. \nhttps://doi.org/10.1108/10878571111161507 \nIman, R. L. (1974). A power study of a rank transform for the 